window.SEARCH_INDEX=[{"t": "Home", "u": "index.html", "p": "Start here", "x": "Everything AI One place for the whole journey: Python, data science, machine learning, deep learning and generative AI. It is written as a study guide, with the ideas explained plainly and the code you actually need. 8 pages Runnable code examples Searchable glossary Press / to search \ud83d\udc0d Python Types"}, {"t": "How the pieces fit together", "u": "index.html#how-the-pieces-fit-together", "p": "Home", "x": "AI is a stack. Each layer relies on the one below it, so the order of the pages is the order that works best for most people. Layer What it gives you Page Python The tool you write everything in Python Math and data skills Arrays, vectors, statistics, clean data, charts Data Science Classical ML Models that learn from tabular data Machine Learning Deep learning Models for images, sequences and lan"}, {"t": "Python", "u": "python.html", "p": "The language of AI: syntax, functions, OOP, threads", "x": "Python Python is the default language of AI because it reads almost like English and has a library for nearly everything. This page covers the parts of the language you will use every day. Getting set up You need three things: a Python interpreter, an editor or IDE, and a way to install packages. Fo"}, {"t": "Getting set up", "u": "python.html#getting-set-up", "p": "Python", "x": "You need three things: a Python interpreter, an editor or IDE, and a way to install packages. For data work, Jupyter Notebook is the usual choice because you can run code in small cells and see results immediately. python --version pip install numpy pandas matplotlib scikit-learn jupyter jupyter notebook Habit worth building Use a virtual environment per project ( python -m venv .venv ) so one pro"}, {"t": "Types, variables and operators", "u": "python.html#types-variables-and-operators", "p": "Python", "x": "Python is dynamically typed: a variable takes the type of the value you assign. The core types are int , float , str , bool , and the collections list , tuple , set and dict . Type Example Mutable? Use it for list [1, 2, 3] Yes Ordered collections that change tuple (1, 2) No Fixed records, dictionary keys set {1, 2, 3} Yes Unique items, fast membership tests dict {\"name\": \"Ada\"} Yes Key-value look"}, {"t": "Decisions and loops", "u": "python.html#decisions-and-loops", "p": "Python", "x": "score = 72 if score >= 90: grade = \"A\" elif score >= 70: grade = \"B\" else: grade = \"C\" for i in range(5): # 0..4 if i == 3: continue # skip this pass if i == 4: break # leave the loop print(i) n = 0 while n < 3: n += 1 else: print(\"loop finished without break\") # runs only if no break break exits the loop, continue skips to the next pass, and the lesser-known else block on a loop runs only when th"}, {"t": "Functions", "u": "python.html#functions", "p": "Python", "x": "A function packages reusable logic. Give it clear inputs and a return value rather than printing inside it. def describe(name, age=30, *tags, **extra): \"\"\"Positional, default, variable-length (*) and keyword (**) arguments.\"\"\" return f\"{name} ({age}) tags={tags} extra={extra}\" describe(\"Ada\", 36, \"ml\", \"math\", city=\"London\") Scope Python looks for a name in this order: L ocal, E nclosing, G lobal,"}, {"t": "Object-oriented programming", "u": "python.html#object-oriented-programming", "p": "Python", "x": "A class is a blueprint and an object is one instance of it. Libraries like scikit-learn and PyTorch are built from classes, so reading them gets much easier once this clicks. class Account: def __init__(self, owner, balance=0): self.owner = owner self._balance = balance # leading underscore: \"treat as private\" def deposit(self, amount): if amount <= 0: raise ValueError(\"amount must be positive\") s"}, {"t": "Threads", "u": "python.html#threads", "p": "Python", "x": "Threads let a program do several things at once, which helps when work spends its time waiting, such as network calls or file reads. Because of the Global Interpreter Lock, threads do not speed up CPU-heavy Python code; use multiprocessing for that. import threading, time def work(n): time.sleep(1) print(\"done\", n) threads = [threading.Thread(target=work, args=(i,)) for i in range(3)] for t in thr"}, {"t": "Files and data formats", "u": "python.html#files-and-data-formats", "p": "Python", "x": "import csv, json with open(\"data.csv\", newline=\"\") as f: rows = list(csv.DictReader(f)) with open(\"out.json\", \"w\") as f: json.dump(rows, f, indent=2) JSON and dictionaries JSON is how most web APIs send data, and a JSON object maps almost one-to-one onto a Python dictionary. If you understand dictionaries, you already understand JSON. This comes up again on the Generative AI page."}, {"t": "Data Science", "u": "data-science.html", "p": "NumPy, linear algebra, statistics, probability, pandas, visualization", "x": "Data Science Data science is the craft of turning raw data into decisions. It rests on four skills: fast numerical computing, a little linear algebra, statistical thinking, and clean tabular data you can chart. The data science process Frame the question. What decision will this inform, and how will"}, {"t": "The data science process", "u": "data-science.html#the-data-science-process", "p": "Data Science", "x": "Frame the question. What decision will this inform, and how will you know it worked? Collect data. Files, databases, APIs, logs. Clean and prepare. Missing values, duplicates, wrong types, outliers. This usually takes the most time. Explore. Summaries and charts to understand distributions and relationships. Model. Statistics or machine learning to answer the question. Communicate and monitor. Sha"}, {"t": "NumPy", "u": "data-science.html#numpy", "p": "Data Science", "x": "NumPy gives you the ndarray , a fixed-type n-dimensional array that runs operations in compiled code. It is far faster than Python lists and is the base of pandas, scikit-learn and the deep learning libraries. import numpy as np a = np.array([[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]]) a.shape # (2, 3): axis 0 = rows, axis 1 = columns a.sum(axis=0) # column sums -> [5, 7, 9] a.mean(axis=1) # row means -> [2., 5.] a[a "}, {"t": "Linear algebra you actually use", "u": "data-science.html#linear-algebra-you-actually-use", "p": "Data Science", "x": "Idea What it is Why it matters Vector An ordered list of numbers One data point with many features Dot product Sum of element-wise products Similarity, and the core of every neuron Norm The length of a vector Distances, regularization Matrix A grid of numbers A whole dataset, or a transformation Transpose Swap rows and columns Aligning shapes for multiplication Rank Number of linearly independent "}, {"t": "Descriptive statistics", "u": "data-science.html#descriptive-statistics", "p": "Data Science", "x": "Types of data Categorical data is nominal (no order, such as colour) or ordinal (ordered, such as small, medium, large). Numerical data is discrete (counts) or continuous (measurements). The type decides which summaries and charts make sense. Centre and spread Measure Use when Mean Data is roughly symmetric with no big outliers Median Data is skewed or has outliers (income, house prices) Mode Cate"}, {"t": "Probability and distributions", "u": "data-science.html#probability-and-distributions", "p": "Data Science", "x": "Distribution Models Example Bernoulli One yes/no trial One coin flip Binomial Number of successes in n trials Heads in 10 flips Poisson Count of events in a fixed interval Support tickets per hour Uniform All outcomes equally likely A fair die Normal Many natural measurements, bell-shaped Heights, measurement error The probability mass function (discrete) or density function (continuous) says how "}, {"t": "Hypothesis testing", "u": "data-science.html#hypothesis-testing", "p": "Data Science", "x": "You start by assuming nothing interesting is happening (the null hypothesis , H0), then ask whether the data is surprising enough to reject that assumption in favour of the alternative (H1). p-value: the probability of seeing data at least this extreme if H0 were true. A small value (commonly below 0.05) is evidence against H0. It is not the probability that H0 is true. Type I error: rejecting a t"}, {"t": "pandas", "u": "data-science.html#pandas", "p": "Data Science", "x": "pandas gives you the Series (one labelled column) and the DataFrame (a table). It is the main tool for loading, cleaning and reshaping data. import pandas as pd df = pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\", parse_dates=[\"date\"]) df.head(); df.info(); df.describe() df[\"revenue\"] = df[\"price\"] * df[\"qty\"] df[df[\"region\"] == \"West\"].sort_values(\"revenue\", ascending=False) df.groupby(\"region\")[\"revenue\"].agg([\"sum\", "}, {"t": "Regular expressions and databases", "u": "data-science.html#regular-expressions-and-databases", "p": "Data Science", "x": "Regular expressions find patterns in text: re.findall(r\"\\d{3}-\\d{4}\", text) . For structured storage, Python's sqlite3 module or pandas.read_sql lets you query a database and get a DataFrame back."}, {"t": "Visualization", "u": "data-science.html#visualization", "p": "Data Science", "x": "Pick the chart for the question, not for the look. Question Chart How is one variable distributed? Histogram, box plot How do two numbers relate? Scatter plot How does something change over time? Line chart How do categories compare? Bar chart How do many variables correlate? Heatmap of the correlation matrix import matplotlib.pyplot as plt import seaborn as sns fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(7, "}, {"t": "Machine Learning", "u": "machine-learning.html", "p": "Regression, classification, clustering, ensembles, recommenders", "x": "Machine Learning Machine learning is software that improves at a task by learning patterns from examples instead of following hand-written rules. This page walks from the core ideas to the algorithms you will reach for most. The big picture Type You give it It learns to Examples Supervised Inputs wi"}, {"t": "The big picture", "u": "machine-learning.html#the-big-picture", "p": "Machine Learning", "x": "Type You give it It learns to Examples Supervised Inputs with known answers (labels) Predict the label for new inputs Price prediction, spam detection Unsupervised Inputs only Find structure Customer segments, anomaly detection Reinforcement An environment and rewards Choose actions that earn more reward Game playing, robotics Supervised problems split in two. If the target is a number (sales next"}, {"t": "The ML pipeline", "u": "machine-learning.html#the-ml-pipeline", "p": "Machine Learning", "x": "Define the problem and the metric that counts as success. Prepare data: clean, encode categories, scale, split. Train one or more models on the training set. Evaluate on data the model has not seen. Tune and compare models and settings. Deploy and monitor (MLOps): track performance, retrain when data drifts."}, {"t": "Generalization: overfitting and underfitting", "u": "machine-learning.html#generalization-overfitting-and-underfitting", "p": "Machine Learning", "x": "A model should do well on new data, not just the data it studied. Underfitting Overfitting Symptom Poor on training and test data Great on training, poor on test Cause Model too simple or too few features Model memorized noise Fixes More capacity, better features More data, simpler model, regularization, cross-validation, early stopping from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split, cross_v"}, {"t": "Regression", "u": "machine-learning.html#regression", "p": "Machine Learning", "x": "Linear regression Fits a straight line (or plane) y = b0 + b1\u00b7x1 + ... by minimizing squared error. It works best when these assumptions roughly hold: a linear relationship, independent errors, constant error variance, errors that are roughly normal, and little multicollinearity between features. from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression from sklearn.metrics import mean_squared_error, r2_s"}, {"t": "Classification", "u": "machine-learning.html#classification", "p": "Machine Learning", "x": "Choosing a metric Metric Answers Prefer when Accuracy What share was right? Classes are balanced Precision Of those flagged positive, how many truly were? False alarms are costly (spam filter) Recall Of all true positives, how many did we catch? Misses are costly (disease screening) F1 Balance of precision and recall Imbalanced classes ROC-AUC How well does it rank positives above negatives? Compa"}, {"t": "Unsupervised learning", "u": "machine-learning.html#unsupervised-learning", "p": "Machine Learning", "x": "Clustering K-means groups points into k clusters: (1) pick k starting centres, (2) assign each point to its nearest centre, (3) move each centre to the mean of its points, (4) repeat until nothing changes. From the live classes Worked by hand in one dimension: with ages and centres 10, 30 and 60, the point 35 is closest to 30 by Euclidean distance (in one dimension that is just the absolute differ"}, {"t": "Ensemble learning", "u": "machine-learning.html#ensemble-learning", "p": "Machine Learning", "x": "Several models together usually beat one. Errors that differ between models cancel out. Method How it combines Main benefit Example Bagging Train in parallel on random resamples; average or vote Reduces variance Random forest Boosting Train in sequence, each fixing the last one's mistakes Reduces bias AdaBoost, Gradient Boosting, XGBoost Stacking A final model learns how to weight the others Squee"}, {"t": "Recommender systems", "u": "machine-learning.html#recommender-systems", "p": "Machine Learning", "x": "Recommenders suggest items a person is likely to want. Content-based: recommend items similar to ones the user liked, using item attributes. Collaborative filtering: use the behaviour of many users. User-based finds similar people and borrows their picks. Item-based finds items often liked together. Model-based: learn hidden factors with matrix factorization (SVD) that explain the rating matrix, t"}, {"t": "Where TensorFlow and PyTorch fit in", "u": "machine-learning.html#where-tensorflow-and-pytorch-fit-in", "p": "Machine Learning", "x": "The same ideas extend to neural networks, covered on the Deep Learning page. TensorFlow with Keras and PyTorch both let you build models layer by layer and train them on a GPU."}, {"t": "Deep Learning", "u": "deep-learning.html", "p": "Neural nets, optimizers, CNNs, RNNs, transformers, PyTorch", "x": "Deep Learning Deep learning uses neural networks with many layers to learn features directly from raw data such as pixels, audio and text. It powers modern vision, speech and language systems. Why deep learning, and when Classical ML needs you to design features by hand. Deep networks learn their ow"}, {"t": "Why deep learning, and when", "u": "deep-learning.html#why-deep-learning-and-when", "p": "Deep Learning", "x": "Classical ML needs you to design features by hand. Deep networks learn their own features, which is why they dominate images, audio and language. The price is that they need lots of data, lots of compute and careful tuning. Classical ML Deep learning Best for Tabular data, small to medium datasets Images, audio, text, very large datasets Features Engineered by you Learned by the network Compute CP"}, {"t": "The perceptron and how networks learn", "u": "deep-learning.html#the-perceptron-and-how-networks-learn", "p": "Deep Learning", "x": "A perceptron is one artificial neuron: multiply each input by a weight, add a bias, and pass the sum through an activation function . z = w1*x1 + w2*x2 + ... + b output = activation(z) Activation Shape Typical use Sigmoid Squashes to 0..1 Binary output probability Tanh Squashes to -1..1 Older recurrent nets ReLU max(0, z) Default for hidden layers Softmax Probabilities that sum to 1 Multi-class ou"}, {"t": "TensorFlow and Keras", "u": "deep-learning.html#tensorflow-and-keras", "p": "Deep Learning", "x": "TensorFlow works on tensors (n-dimensional arrays). Keras is its friendly model-building layer. Use the Sequential API for a simple stack of layers and the Functional API for branches, multiple inputs or outputs. TensorFlow Playground is a free browser tool for building intuition on tiny networks. import tensorflow as tf from tensorflow import keras model = keras.Sequential([ keras.layers.Input(sh"}, {"t": "Optimizers", "u": "deep-learning.html#optimizers", "p": "Deep Learning", "x": "An optimizer decides how to use the gradient. They build on each other: Optimizer Key idea SGD Step downhill using a small batch of data at a time Momentum Keep a running velocity so steps smooth out and roll through flat spots Adagrad Give rarely-updated parameters bigger steps Adadelta, RMSProp Adapt step sizes using a moving window of recent gradients, fixing Adagrad's ever-shrinking steps Adam"}, {"t": "Making training work", "u": "deep-learning.html#making-training-work", "p": "Deep Learning", "x": "Batch normalization rescales each layer's inputs, which speeds training and tolerates higher learning rates. Vanishing and exploding gradients: in deep nets gradients can shrink to nothing or blow up. ReLU, careful weight initialization, normalization, residual connections and gradient clipping help. Dropout randomly switches off neurons during training so the network cannot lean on any one of the"}, {"t": "Convolutional neural networks (CNNs)", "u": "deep-learning.html#convolutional-neural-networks-cnns", "p": "Deep Learning", "x": "CNNs are built for images. Instead of connecting every pixel to every neuron, small filters slide across the image and detect local patterns such as edges, then textures, then object parts in later layers. Filters are shared across the image, which cuts parameters dramatically. Layer Does Convolution Applies filters to produce feature maps Pooling (max or average) Shrinks the maps and adds toleran"}, {"t": "Transfer learning", "u": "deep-learning.html#transfer-learning", "p": "Deep Learning", "x": "Instead of training from scratch, start from a model pretrained on a huge dataset (for example ImageNet) and adapt it. Freeze the early layers, which already know edges and shapes, and train a new final layer on your small dataset. Benefits: far less data and compute, and usually better accuracy. Pick a pretrained model that matches your task and image size, and check its licence. base = keras.app"}, {"t": "Object detection and edge deployment", "u": "deep-learning.html#object-detection-and-edge-deployment", "p": "Deep Learning", "x": "Classification says what is in an image; object detection also says where, by predicting bounding boxes. YOLO (\"you only look once\") does this in a single pass, which makes it fast enough for video. Preparing the dataset means labelling boxes for each object. For phones and embedded devices, TensorFlow Lite converts a trained model into a smaller, faster format."}, {"t": "Sequence models: RNNs", "u": "deep-learning.html#sequence-models-rnns", "p": "Deep Learning", "x": "Some data is ordered: text, speech, sensor readings. A recurrent neural network reads one step at a time and carries a hidden state forward as memory. Training uses backpropagation through time. Plain RNNs forget over long spans because of vanishing gradients, which is why LSTM and GRU cells with gates were invented. Hybrid models combine a CNN to read each frame with an RNN to read the sequence, "}, {"t": "Transformers and BERT", "u": "deep-learning.html#transformers-and-bert", "p": "Deep Learning", "x": "Transformers replaced recurrence with self-attention : every token looks at every other token and decides how much to weigh it. That captures long-range context and trains in parallel on GPUs. Architecture: an encoder stack, a decoder stack, positional encoding (so order is known), multi-head attention and feed-forward layers. BERT uses only the encoder and reads text in both directions. It is pre"}, {"t": "Autoencoders", "u": "deep-learning.html#autoencoders", "p": "Deep Learning", "x": "An autoencoder squeezes its input through a narrow bottleneck (the encoder) then rebuilds it (the decoder), trained to make the output match the input. The bottleneck is forced to hold the essence of the data. Uses: compression, denoising, anomaly detection (bad reconstruction means unusual input) and pretraining."}, {"t": "PyTorch", "u": "deep-learning.html#pytorch", "p": "Deep Learning", "x": "PyTorch builds the computation graph as your code runs, which makes debugging feel like normal Python. You write the model as a class and the training loop yourself. import torch, torch.nn as nn class Net(nn.Module): def __init__(self): super().__init__() self.net = nn.Sequential(nn.Linear(20, 64), nn.ReLU(), nn.Linear(64, 1)) def forward(self, x): return self.net(x) model, loss_fn = Net(), nn.BCE"}, {"t": "Generative AI", "u": "generative-ai.html", "p": "LLMs, prompt engineering, APIs, limits and cost", "x": "Generative AI Generative AI creates new content (text, images, code, audio) rather than only labelling or predicting. This page covers how the main model families work, how to steer them with prompts, how to call them from code, and where the real limits are. What makes it \"generative\" A discriminat"}, {"t": "What makes it \"generative\"", "u": "generative-ai.html#what-makes-it-generative", "p": "Generative AI", "x": "A discriminative model answers \"which category is this?\". A generative model learns the shape of the data itself well enough to produce new samples that look like it. The ideas build directly on deep learning . Model family How it generates Typical output Transformer-based (LLMs) Predicts the next token repeatedly, using attention over the whole context Text, code, summaries, translation Diffusion"}, {"t": "How large language models work", "u": "generative-ai.html#how-large-language-models-work", "p": "Generative AI", "x": "Tokens: text is split into small pieces (words or parts of words). Models read and write tokens, and usage is usually billed per token. Pretraining: the model reads huge amounts of text and learns to predict the next token. This is where its broad knowledge and fluency come from. Fine-tuning and instruction tuning: further training on examples of good behaviour, so the model follows instructions, "}, {"t": "Prompt engineering", "u": "generative-ai.html#prompt-engineering", "p": "Generative AI", "x": "The prompt is your main control over the output. Good prompting is clear writing, not magic words. Zero-shot, few-shot and beyond Technique What you do Use it when Zero-shot Give an instruction with no examples The task is simple and common Few-shot Include a few worked examples inside the prompt so the model copies the pattern and style You need a specific format, tone or label set Role / persona"}, {"t": "Calling a model from Python", "u": "generative-ai.html#calling-a-model-from-python", "p": "Generative AI", "x": "Hosted models are reached through an API . You authenticate with an API key , send a list of messages as JSON, and read the reply. The same pattern works across providers (OpenAI, Groq, Anthropic, and others); only the client and model name change. import os, json from groq import Groq # pip install groq client = Groq(api_key=os.environ[\"GROQ_API_KEY\"]) # never hard-code keys prompt = \"\"\"Extract t"}, {"t": "Giving a model your own knowledge: RAG", "u": "generative-ai.html#giving-a-model-your-own-knowledge-rag", "p": "Generative AI", "x": "Retrieval-augmented generation keeps the base model unchanged and hands it your data at question time. The flow is: split your documents into chunks, turn each into an embedding (a vector of numbers capturing meaning), store them in a vector index, and when a question arrives, find the closest chunks and put them in the prompt. This gives domain-specific, up-to-date answers without retraining, and"}, {"t": "Agentic AI", "u": "generative-ai.html#agentic-ai", "p": "Generative AI", "x": "A standard model answers a prompt. An agent goes further: it plans, picks tools (search, code, email, databases), carries out steps and checks the result, with limited human intervention. An onboarding agent, for example, could read a request, create accounts, send emails and log what it did. Self-driving cars are a physical-world cousin. The more freedom an agent has, the more it needs permission"}, {"t": "Responsible AI: bias, privacy and security", "u": "generative-ai.html#responsible-ai-bias-privacy-and-security", "p": "Generative AI", "x": "Bias. Models reflect their training data. If the data under-represents or stereotypes a group, outputs will too. Mitigate with diverse, representative data, testing across groups, human review, and reporting problems to the model provider. RAG over vetted data can also help steer outputs. Privacy. Do not paste confidential or personal data into tools you do not control. Privacy-preserving approach"}, {"t": "Will AI replace jobs?", "u": "generative-ai.html#will-ai-replace-jobs", "p": "Generative AI", "x": "The realistic view from the class discussion: generative AI changes how work is done more than it removes whole roles. It speeds up drafting, coding and analysis, so a person can act as a reviewer who validates the output rather than writing everything from scratch. Reasons it does not simply replace people: Cost. Running large models at scale is expensive, so automating everything is often unecon"}, {"t": "A quick checklist before you ship an AI feature", "u": "generative-ai.html#a-quick-checklist-before-you-ship-an-ai-feature", "p": "Generative AI", "x": "Is the prompt specific, with a defined output format? Are outputs validated before anything acts on them? Is a person in the loop for high-stakes decisions? Are keys, personal data and secrets kept out of prompts and code? Have you tested for bias and for failure cases? Do you know the cost per request at your expected volume?"}, {"t": "Project Playbook", "u": "playbook.html", "p": "A practical checklist from the live classes", "x": "Project Playbook A practical, end-to-end checklist for taking a dataset to a model you can trust. It pulls together the habits from the lessons and the live classes into one workflow. 1. Frame the problem Write the question in one sentence and say what decision it supports. Classify it: regression, "}, {"t": "1. Frame the problem", "u": "playbook.html#1-frame-the-problem", "p": "Project Playbook", "x": "Write the question in one sentence and say what decision it supports. Classify it: regression, classification, clustering, recommendation, or generation. Pick the metric that matches the cost of mistakes (see the metrics table ). Decide the baseline to beat: a simple rule, the average, or the current process."}, {"t": "2. Inspect the data", "u": "playbook.html#2-inspect-the-data", "p": "Project Playbook", "x": "df.shape; df.dtypes; df.head() df.isna().mean().sort_values(ascending=False).head(10) # worst missing columns df.describe(include=\"all\").T df.duplicated().sum() Look at the target's distribution first. Check class balance before anything else."}, {"t": "3. Clean and prepare", "u": "playbook.html#3-clean-and-prepare", "p": "Project Playbook", "x": "Remove columns with no information. Zero-variance columns (a single unique value) cannot help any model. Classify columns automatically by unique-value count: binary, low-cardinality categorical, high-cardinality, numeric. Handle missing values deliberately: drop, fill with median or mode, or add an \"is missing\" flag. Encode categories (one-hot for few levels, target or ordinal encoding for many)."}, {"t": "4. Build a baseline, then compare", "u": "playbook.html#4-build-a-baseline-then-compare", "p": "Project Playbook", "x": "Start with something simple: logistic or linear regression. It is fast and sets the bar. Try a tree-based ensemble (random forest, gradient boosting). They are strong defaults on tabular data. Compare models on the same split or cross-validation folds, using the metric from step 1. from sklearn.model_selection import cross_validate models = {\"logreg\": LogisticRegression(max_iter=1000), \"rf\": Rando"}, {"t": "5. Diagnose", "u": "playbook.html#5-diagnose", "p": "Project Playbook", "x": "You see Likely cause Try Train and validation both poor Underfitting Richer features, more flexible model Train great, validation poor Overfitting More data, regularization, simpler model, early stopping Great accuracy, bad recall on the rare class Imbalance Better metric, class weights, SMOTE on train only Test score far above anything realistic Data leakage Check that no feature reveals the answ"}, {"t": "6. Tune and evaluate once", "u": "playbook.html#6-tune-and-evaluate-once", "p": "Project Playbook", "x": "Tune hyperparameters with cross-validation on the training data. Touch the test set once, at the very end, to get an honest final number. Report the metric together with a confusion matrix and a sentence on where the model fails."}, {"t": "7. Explain it", "u": "playbook.html#7-explain-it", "p": "Project Playbook", "x": "Show feature importance or SHAP values. Give one or two concrete example predictions, right and wrong. State the limits: what data it was trained on, and where it should not be trusted."}, {"t": "8. Ship and monitor", "u": "playbook.html#8-ship-and-monitor", "p": "Project Playbook", "x": "Save the whole pipeline ( joblib.dump(pipe, \"model.joblib\") ), not just the model, so preprocessing travels with it. Log inputs and predictions. Watch for data drift (inputs change) and concept drift (the relationship changes). Set a retraining trigger and keep the previous version for rollback."}, {"t": "Study plan", "u": "playbook.html#study-plan", "p": "Project Playbook", "x": "Week Focus Proof you got it 1 Python : functions, classes, files Write a script that cleans a CSV 2 Data science : NumPy, pandas, statistics Explore a dataset and run a t-test 3-4 Machine learning : supervised models and metrics Beat a baseline with cross-validation 5 Clustering and dimensionality reduction Segment customers and explain the groups 6-7 Deep learning : networks, CNNs, transfer learn"}, {"t": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html", "p": "Searchable definitions", "x": "Glossary Plain-English definitions. Type in the box to filter. Activation function A function applied to a neuron's weighted sum that lets a network model non-linear patterns. Common ones: ReLU, sigmoid, tanh, softmax. Agentic AI An AI system that plans and carries out multi-step tasks using tools, "}];